WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.655
This is an atlas of 582 papers from three chip-design conferences: ISCA, DAC and ICCAD. It asks one question.

2
00:00:08.655 --> 00:00:15.359
What do these papers, taken together, say about where chip and computer design is going?

3
00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:22.067
Here is the whole story in three sentences. One: chip design is becoming a loop.

4
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:29.049
Propose, check, learn, try again. Two: accelerators, memory and reliability draw the most papers.

5
00:00:29.049 --> 00:00:36.031
Three: most papers were read from short descriptions, so these are hypotheses, not proven trends.

6
00:00:36.981 --> 00:00:41.584
The atlas works in five steps. Start with 582 paper records.

7
00:00:41.584 --> 00:00:48.566
Sort them into 10 broad themes, then 113 narrower subthemes, each explained in plain words.

8
00:00:48.566 --> 00:00:56.468
Read some papers in full. Then ask which directions keep coming back, and what test would confirm them.

9
00:00:57.418 --> 00:01:06.050
First, where the papers come from. DAC, the Design Automation Conference, supplies 544 of the 582.

10
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:12.480
ISCA, the main computer architecture conference, adds 17 selected papers.

11
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:21.728
ICCAD's 2026 papers were not public yet, so 21 come from 2025. So the atlas leans toward design software.

12
00:01:22.678 --> 00:01:30.400
Here is where the attention goes. Architecture and accelerators is the largest theme, with 103 papers.

13
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.609
Memory, interconnect, and storage has 85. Power, reliability, and security has 78.

14
00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:43.271
A count shows where this sample spends its attention. It is not a ranking of importance.

15
00:01:44.221 --> 00:01:48.745
The common thread is a loop. A model or a search proposes a design.

16
00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:54.213
Tools check it: does it compile, does it simulate correctly, does it meet timing?

17
00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:58.399
The check reports what failed, and the next attempt uses that.

18
00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:03.800
The hard question is cost: a fair test counts every model call, retry and check.

19
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.092
The second direction is about data.

20
00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:13.716
A chip can have thousands of arithmetic units, but they wait if their inputs cannot arrive in time.

21
00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:20.072
So many papers decide where data sits, how far it moves, and whether to compute next to memory.

22
00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:24.622
The test is the end-to-end result, with every copy and wait counted.

23
00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:29.137
Two more directions move physics and safety earlier.

24
00:02:29.137 --> 00:02:33.525
Heat, wire delay and power supply used to be checked at the end.

25
00:02:33.525 --> 00:02:39.764
Now fast estimates guide early choices, but the final design still needs an accurate check.

26
00:02:39.764 --> 00:02:46.415
Likewise, faults and attacks are named first, and the cost of protection is reported, not hidden.

27
00:02:47.365 --> 00:02:50.350
The last direction is about measurement itself.

28
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:56.447
A score means little without the tasks it was tested on, the rule for success, and the baseline.

29
00:02:56.447 --> 00:03:00.067
More papers now build benchmarks and release their tools.

30
00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:06.291
The test for this direction is simple: can someone else repeat the result and get the same answer?

31
00:03:07.241 --> 00:03:15.470
Now, how sure can we be? 541 of the 582 records were coded from the short official program description.

32
00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:22.501
Only 33 come from a full paper, preprint or author PDF. 16 papers were checked in depth.

33
00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:27.934
So every direction here is a hypothesis to test, not a proven trend.

34
00:03:28.884 --> 00:03:35.800
Here are the six directions to watch. Requirements checked while designing. Data movement as the real cost.

35
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.264
Measure, then decide the next design. Faults and attacks planned early. Physical limits in the loop.

36
00:03:42.264 --> 00:03:49.375
And better ways to measure. Each page names the paper that pushes back, and the result that would disprove it.

37
00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:53.197
To explore it yourself, start with a theme.

38
00:03:53.197 --> 00:04:00.413
Each one explains the technical problem in plain words, then links to its subthemes and every paper in them.

39
00:04:00.413 --> 00:04:05.424
Check the deep reads for papers read in full. Then open the six directions.

40
00:04:05.424 --> 00:04:08.565
Everything is linked from the big picture page.
